--- commit messages
=== 86fe59e6bf07108142fb3dbd06bef8747d2a4687
KS-844: KS-727 guard header states corpus 1's counts as they now are (comment only)

The header said "8 modules contributing 9 handlers", "The 9th handler" and
"Of those 9". After this PR registered demo-service's errorHandler, the
counts are 9, 10th and 10.

How counted: parsed the exact sets at the previous head e28c91f9b from this
file.
- EXPECTED_CORPUS has 9 entries.
- EXPECTED_HANDLERS has 10 entries across 9 distinct modules. One module
  (services/api-gateway/src/middleware/errorHandler.ts) contributes two
  handlers, errorHandler and payloadTooLargeErrorHandler.
- FORWARDING_HANDLERS is unchanged, so "exactly 1 is a FILTER" stands.

Three comment lines changed; no code; ks727 still 103/103.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

=== e28c91f9b990273ea34036cb88ad6abf0f39e73b
KS-844: demo-service answers errors as JSON through an exported errorHandler (not express's HTML page)

demo-service mounted no error middleware, so a body the JSON parser refuses
(or any thrown route) got express's default HTML page, with a stack trace and
absolute paths.

- New services/demo-service/src/middleware/errorHandler.ts exports
  `errorHandler`, a four-arg JSON handler:
  - 400 -> BAD_REQUEST, 413 -> PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE, other 4xx ->
    REQUEST_ERROR, all with the error's own message;
  - 5xx -> INTERNAL_ERROR with a fixed "Internal server error".
- app.ts mounts it last, after the 404 handler.
- It is an exported module, not inline in app.ts: the KS-727 class guard's
  corpus 2 forbids a terminal inline handler in a service entrypoint, and
  the exported form is driven by corpus 1 across every NODE_ENV with a leak
  canary.

Guard edits (packages/shared, Wednesday's conditions):
- ks727-errorhandler-class-guard.test.ts: exactly one new entry in corpus
  1's EXPECTED_CORPUS and EXPECTED_HANDLERS; nothing else changed. Corpus 2
  is untouched and green.
- ks781-p3-3-body-parser-order.test.ts (KS-832 acceptance bar): its header
  assumed demo-service mounts no error handler of its own. The marker layer
  is now spliced into the router stack ahead of demo-service's errorHandler.
  The control still proves "refused by the JSON parser" by entity.parse.failed.

Test file ks844-demo-service-mounts-no-error-handler.test.ts: the READY's
three cells, with the parsed body typed (three TS18046 under a type-check
that includes tests).

Test cells and the handler body authored by the local model (Ornith) from a
Wednesday-written brief; the extraction and guard edits are seat A's, on
Wednesday's ruling.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

